{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 229,
    "dDurationMs": 2041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why is the sky dark at night?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2270,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might think the answer is obvious - the\nsun isn't up! But the only reason the sky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6330,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks blue during the day is that sunlight\nscatters off of the atmosphere - if we didn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10869,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have an atmosphere (like on the moon), the\nsky would always be dark, even when the sun"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15239,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is shining. So let’s rephrase the question\n- why is space dark?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19369,
    "dDurationMs": 4371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, space is full of stars - countless\nstars which are all about as bright as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23740,
    "dDurationMs": 4209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun, and in an infinite eternal universe,\nno matter what direction you picked, if you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27949,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looked far enough in that direction, you would\nsee a star or galaxy. So the whole sky should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32860,
    "dDurationMs": 2949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be as bright as the sun, night and day!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35809,
    "dDurationMs": 3561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And since it's not, does the darkness of the\nnight sky mean that there's some distance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39370,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away from us when stars and galaxies just…\nstop? A boundary between something and nothing?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45070,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An \"edge\" to the universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46930,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not exactly - all of our evidence seems to\nindicate that space has no edge. But the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53519,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself does - not a spatial edge, but a temporal\none: as far as we know, the universe had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59859,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a beginning. Or at least, a time about 13.7\nbillion years ago when the universe was so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64470,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small and crumpled-up with itself that our\nstandard notion of space and time breaks down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69610,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And since only a finite amount of time has\npassed since this so-called beginning, that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73480,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that some of the stars necessary to\nfill up the \"brightness in every direction\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77250,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are so far away that light from them plain\nhasn't had time to reach us yet… it's as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81290,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the universe were a big thunderstorm and\nwe're still waiting to hear the thunder from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84880,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the really distant stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86640,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wait, it’s better than that - since\nlight takes time to travel across the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91000,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we point our telescopes at something\nreally far away, we're actually seeing that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94470,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of universe as it was when the light\nwas emitted. So when we look at 13.5 billion-year-old"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99750,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light, it's not that we don't see stars just\nbecause light from them hasn't gotten to us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103390,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet - we don't see any stars because we’re\ngetting a peek at the universe before any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107530,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars had formed! A star-less universe! Now\nthat sounds to me like a pretty good reason"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112290,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why we look up and see a dark night sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114450,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But… it’s not. I mean, it is true that\nwe can find points in the sky where there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119390,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren’t any stars by looking past the earliest\nstars and thus farther back in time. But even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124090,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we point our telescopes past the earliest\nstars, we still see light. Not starlight,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129929,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the light left over from the big bang.\nAnd we detect this “cosmic background radiation”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134489,
    "dDurationMs": 4821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming more or less evenly from all directions,\nforming a background beyond the stars. So,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139310,
    "dDurationMs": 3769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I guess the night sky ISN’T actually dark\nto begin with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143079,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right... so if our telescopes tell us that\nthe night sky isn’t dark, then why does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148099,
    "dDurationMs": 991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it look dark?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149090,
    "dDurationMs": 4319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s a clue to the real answer: when the\nHubble telescope photographed the distant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153409,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars of the astoundingly beautiful Hubble\nextreme deep field, it took the picture using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157999,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an infrared camera. Why? Well, distant stars\nand galaxies are moving away from us because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163409,
    "dDurationMs": 4371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe is expanding. So the same way\na record slowing down lowers the pitch of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167780,
    "dDurationMs": 4959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "my voice, the doppler effect causes stars\nmoving away from us to become redder, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172739,
    "dDurationMs": 3961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the farther away they are, the faster they\nmove away from us and the redder they become,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176700,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until they become… infrared. And then we\ncan't see them any more. At least not with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181730,
    "dDurationMs": 4429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our human eyes - and that’s why the night\nsky appears dark!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186159,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In summary: If we lived in an infinite, unchanging\nuniverse, the entire sky would be as bright"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191499,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the sun. But the sky is dark at night,\nboth because the universe had a beginning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195400,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so there aren't stars in every direction and\nmore importantly because the light from super"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199230,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distant stars (and the even-more-distant cosmic\nbackground radiation) gets red-shifted away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203840,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the visible spectrum by the expansion\nof the universe, so we just plain can't see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207939,
    "dDurationMs": 571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208510,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally! We’ve shed some light on why the\nnight sky is dark. And why it isn’t."
    } ]
  } ]
}
